{% comment %}
  The round TABI seal: a brand name held still in the middle while a ring of
  text turns slowly around it.

  Drawn rather than uploaded, so the wording stays a theme setting and the mark
  stays sharp at any size. Reproducing the supplied logo needs three things:

  - Two arcs, not one ring. Both halves of the artwork read left to right - the
    top half with its letters' tops pointing up and out, the bottom half with
    theirs pointing down and out. That is two arcs drawn in opposite directions
    between the same two points (9 o'clock and 3 o'clock), not one continuous
    circle, whose bottom half would run right to left instead.
  - `textLength` on each arc. Each half is told to occupy exactly the same
    span of arc, and the browser distributes the letter spacing to fill it. So
    a merchant can retype either line and the ring stays evenly set instead of
    drifting off centre or colliding with a star - which fixed tracking, sized
    once for one particular string, cannot promise.
  - The two four-pointed stars sitting on the seam between the arcs, at 9 and
    3 o'clock, which is where the artwork puts them.

  Everything is laid out in a 200x200 viewBox and scaled by CSS, so the numbers
  below are proportions of the mark, not pixels.

  Accepts:
  - name:      {String} the still word in the middle
  - text_top:  {String} the arc over it
  - text_bot:  {String} the arc under it
  - speed:     {Number} seconds for one full turn
  - direction: {String} 'anticlockwise' turns it the other way
  - uid:       {String} unique suffix for the arc ids - a page can hold more
               than one band, and duplicate ids would point every seal's text
               at the first seal's arcs

  The mark is hidden from assistive tech: it repeats the brand name and the
  band's own copy, so announcing it again is noise rather than information.

  Usage:
  {% render 'pdp-seal', name: 'Tabi', text_top: 'A place to belong', uid: section.id %}
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign seal_name = name | default: 'Tabi'
  assign seal_top = text_top | default: ''
  assign seal_bot = text_bot | default: ''
  assign seal_speed = speed | default: 24
  assign seal_uid = uid | default: 'pdp'

  assign spin = 'normal'
  if direction == 'anticlockwise'
    assign spin = 'reverse'
  endif

  comment
    Size the middle word to the space inside the ring.

    A fixed size only works for a word the length of the one it was set for.
    "TABI" at 30 sits comfortably inside; a six-letter name at 30 runs straight
    through the ring text and out over the stars, which is what the artwork
    does not survive.

    The estimate: a capital in this face advances about 0.62 of the font size
    and the tracking below adds 0.16 between letters, so n letters occupy
    roughly `size * (0.78n - 0.16)`. Solving that against a target width gives
    the largest size that still fits, and 30 remains the cap so short names
    keep the drawn size rather than growing to fill the circle.

    The target is 92, not the 129 units of clear space actually between the two
    stars. 92 is what "TABI" occupies at the drawn size of 30, so every other
    length is fitted to the same measure the artwork uses rather than to the
    last pixel before a collision - at 129 a six-letter name technically fits
    and visibly crowds the stars either side of it.

    Arithmetic is scaled by 100 throughout, because Liquid divides integers.
  endcomment
  assign name_size = 30
  if seal_name != blank
    assign name_span = seal_name | size | times: 78 | minus: 16
    assign name_fit = 9200 | divided_by: name_span
    if name_fit < 30
      assign name_size = name_fit
    endif
  endif
-%}

<svg
  class="pdp-seal"
  viewBox="0 0 200 200"
  xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
  aria-hidden="true"
  focusable="false"
  style="--pdp-seal-speed: {{ seal_speed }}s; --pdp-seal-spin: {{ spin }}; --pdp-seal-name-size: {{ name_size }}px;"
>
  <defs>
    {%- comment -%}
      Same two endpoints, opposite sweep flags. Flag 1 arcs over the top and
      flag 0 under the bottom, which is what lets both lines read left to
      right. Radius 70 puts the baseline where the artwork has it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <path id="pdp-seal-top-{{ seal_uid }}" fill="none" d="M 30,100 A 70,70 0 0 1 170,100"/>
    <path id="pdp-seal-bot-{{ seal_uid }}" fill="none" d="M 30,100 A 70,70 0 0 0 170,100"/>
  </defs>

  <g class="pdp-seal__ring">
    {%- comment -%}
      175 of the 220 units each half-arc runs, centred on it - so roughly 20
      degrees of clear arc is left at each end for the stars to sit in.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if seal_top != blank -%}
      <text class="pdp-seal__arc">
        <textPath
          href="#pdp-seal-top-{{ seal_uid }}"
          startOffset="50%"
          text-anchor="middle"
          textLength="175"
          lengthAdjust="spacing"
        >{{ seal_top | escape }}</textPath>
      </text>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if seal_bot != blank -%}
      <text class="pdp-seal__arc">
        <textPath
          href="#pdp-seal-bot-{{ seal_uid }}"
          startOffset="50%"
          text-anchor="middle"
          textLength="175"
          lengthAdjust="spacing"
        >{{ seal_bot | escape }}</textPath>
      </text>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      The four-pointed stars, on the seam at 9 and 3 o'clock. One path drawn
      about its own origin and placed twice, so the two cannot drift apart.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <path class="pdp-seal__star" transform="translate(30 100)" d="M 0,-5.4 Q 0.7,-0.7 5.4,0 Q 0.7,0.7 0,5.4 Q -0.7,0.7 -5.4,0 Q -0.7,-0.7 0,-5.4 Z"/>
    <path class="pdp-seal__star" transform="translate(170 100)" d="M 0,-5.4 Q 0.7,-0.7 5.4,0 Q 0.7,0.7 0,5.4 Q -0.7,0.7 -5.4,0 Q -0.7,-0.7 0,-5.4 Z"/>
  </g>

  {%- comment -%}
    Outside the turning group, so it stays upright while the ring moves.
    `dy` rather than dominant-baseline: it centres cap-height text the same way
    in every engine, where dominant-baseline still varies.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if seal_name != blank -%}
    <text class="pdp-seal__name" x="100" y="100" dy="0.35em" text-anchor="middle">
      {{- seal_name | escape -}}
    </text>
  {%- endif -%}
</svg>
